::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q09::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 8^(2/3)? {
=(cuberoot(8))^2 #Correct.
~(sqrt(8))^3 #Not quite. The root and power are reversed.
~8^2/3 #Not quite. The exponent is not division.
~8 + 2/3 #Not quite. The exponent is not addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q10::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 11^(4/5)? {
~(fourthroot(11))^5 #Not quite. The root and power are reversed.
=(fifthroot(11))^4 #Correct.
~11^5/4 #Not quite. The exponent is not division.
~11^(9/5) #Not quite. The exponent was incorrectly changed.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q11::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 2^(1/4) * 2^(3/4). {
~2^(3/16) #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~4 #Not quite. The base was incorrectly changed.
=2 #Correct.
~2^(4/8) #Not quite. The fraction was not simplified correctly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q12::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 9^(5/2) / 9^(1/2). {
~9^3 #Not quite. The exponents were not subtracted correctly.
~9^(6/2) #Not quite. Exponents were added instead of subtracted.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal.
=9^2 #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q13::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 7^(2/3) * 7^(4/3). {
=7^2 #Correct.
~7^(8/9) #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~14^(6/3) #Not quite. The base was incorrectly changed.
~7^(2/3) #Not quite. One factor was ignored.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q14::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 10^(7/5) / 10^(2/5). {
~10^(9/5) #Not quite. Exponents were added instead of subtracted.
=10 #Correct.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal.
~10^(14/25) #Not quite. Exponents were incorrectly multiplied.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q15::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] What does the denominator in 81^(3/4) tell you to do? {
~Raise to the third power. #Not quite. This is the numeratorâ€™s role.
~Divide by 4. #Not quite. The denominator does not indicate division.
=Take the fourth root. #Correct.
~Multiply by 4. #Not quite. The denominator defines the root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q16::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] What does the numerator in 32^(2/5) tell you to do? {
~Take the fifth root. #Not quite. This is the denominatorâ€™s role.
~Divide by 2. #Not quite. The numerator is not division.
~Multiply by 2. #Not quite. The numerator defines a power.
=Raise to the second power. #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q17::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] A student evaluates 64^(2/3) as 64^2/3. Which correction is best? {
=Use the cube root first, then apply the power. #Correct.
~Divide by 3, then square. #Not quite. The denominator defines a root.
~Square first and ignore the denominator. #Not quite. Both parts matter.
~Reverse the exponent. #Not quite. This changes the value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q18::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression has the same value as sqrt(50)? {
~50^2 #Not quite. This squares instead of taking a root.
=50^(1/2) #Correct.
~50^(1/3) #Not quite. This is a cube root.
~50/2 #Not quite. A root is not division.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q19::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression has the same value as cuberoot(19)? {
~19^3 #Not quite. This cubes instead of taking a root.
~19^(1/2) #Not quite. This is a square root.
=19^(1/3) #Correct.
~19/3 #Not quite. A root is not division.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q20::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to (4^(1/2))^6? {
~4^(1/12) #Not quite. Exponents are multiplied, not divided.
~24^(1/2) #Not quite. The base was incorrectly changed.
~4^(6+1/2) #Not quite. Exponents are multiplied, not added.
=4^3 #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q21::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 10000^(1/4). {
=10 #Correct.
~100 #Not quite. This uses a square root instead of a fourth root.
~2500 #Not quite. This treats the exponent as division.
~4 #Not quite. The denominator names the root, not the value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q22::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 27^(4/3)? {
~(fourthroot(27))^3 #Not quite. The root and power are reversed.
=(cuberoot(27))^4 #Correct.
~27^4/3 #Not quite. The exponent is not division.
~27^(1/12) #Not quite. The exponent was incorrectly changed.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q23::
