::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q24::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which explanation correctly justifies 5^(1/2) * 5^(1/2)=5? {
~The bases add to 10, then divide by 2. #Not quite. Bases are not added.
~The exponents multiply to 1. #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~Each factor equals 5. #Not quite. 5^(1/2) is not 5.
=Same base multiplication means add exponents, and 1/2+1/2=1. #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q25::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 64^(1/2)? {
=(sqrt(64)) #Correct.
~(cuberoot(64)) #Not quite. This corresponds to an exponent of 1/3.
~64^2 #Not quite. This squares the number instead of taking a root.
~64/2 #Not quite. A rational exponent does not mean division.
}
